    "bread_config_repoint": "03_operations/page_generator/configs/bread.json baseline_json re-pointed from bread_frontend_v3.json (stale, unserved) to bread_frontend_v4.json (the actual live/served file) -- was pointing PD-build sourcing at the wrong file. v3 itself left untouched/undeleted (still referenced by a few legacy admin/SEO utility scripts outside this pipeline).",
    "bread_v3_v4_resync": "03_operations/bsip1/task629_corrected/sync_bread_v3.py re-applied the identical whitelist correction (score/grade/confidence*/insightLine/rowVerdict/expansion.nutrition) to the 22 correctable barcodes bread_frontend_v3.json shares with v4 (excl. 7290016967074), restoring the pre-existing documented invariant '23 survivors byte-identical between v3 and v4' that this task's v4-only correction would otherwise have broken. v3's rank/_hash_no_rank recomputed for all 29 v3 products; the 6 v3-only crackers-split products (barcodes 74252, 96086000966, 96086000577, 7296073134459, 7296073134442, 8434165658523) were NOT touched.",
    "pd_stale_file_cleanup": "6 stale bread dossier files from a prior (pre-config-repoint) build_dossiers.py run were deleted before the final rebuild+sync so the PD index reflects only the current 23-product bread corpus."
  },
  "critical_catch_shared_parser_reverify": {
    "what": "Before finalizing, every one of the 79 corrected-capture records (bread 23 + crackers 19 + cheese 37, all with status=scraped and a persisted nutrition_raw_source.rows block) was RE-DERIVED independently from its raw rows through the shared, TASK-621-hardened parser chain (parse_nutrition_rows -> bare_to_raw_keys -> parse_nutrition_numeric in 03_operations/bsip0/scrape/_shared/bsip0_nutrition.py) and cross-checked against the capture file's own precomputed nutrition_numeric field, via 03_operations/bsip1/task629_corrected/reverify_via_shared_parser.py.",
    "finding": "1/79 mismatch: crackers barcode 7290018790328 (קרקר מרובע מלוח, a SALTED cracker) carried sodium_raw='1,200 מג' (1200 mg) in its raw row, but the capture file's own precomputed nutrition_numeric.sodium_mg was 1.2 -- the exact TASK-621 comma-thousands bug, still present in whatever produced this capture's canonicalization_schema='task616_type_b_rows_reconstructed_from_nutrition_raw'. This is NOT the same code path TASK-621 patched (a different, capture-canonicalization-side reconstruction), so it was not caught by that fix. Using the trusted-but-wrong 1.2mg would have UNDER-corrected the score (inflating it) and produced a FALSE SUPERLATIVE claim caught downstream by validate_comparison_page.py's rank_check.py ('claims max sodium (own=1.2) but the max is 754.0 held by a different product').",
    "fix_applied": "03_operations/bsip1/task629_corrected/crackers/output/bsip1_7290018790328.json patched to sodium_mg=1200.0 (re-derived value); re-scored via rescore_all.py (new score 35.0/D vs the wrong-sodium score of 44.2/D vs originally-published 50.3/C); crackers_frontend_v1.json's entry for this barcode corrected to score=35.0, expansion.nutrition.sodium=1200.0 (grade stays D, unchanged from the already-applied C->D transition, so no additional PENDING_COPY churn). Post-fix: shared-parser reverify shows 0/79 mismatches, and validate_comparison_page.py's superlative check flips from '2 false' to '0 false'.",
    "escalation_note_to_capture_canonicalize_lane": "The capture-canonicalize agent/lane should be told its task616_type_b_rows_reconstructed_from_nutrition_raw canonicalization schema is NOT routing nutrition_numeric through the shared parser and can reproduce the comma-thousands defect independently of TASK-621's fix. This was a one-instance catch in a 79-record sample, not a corpus-wide audit of that schema."
  },
